 "ssm": {
  "arm_id": "seq_ssm",
  "label": "State-space model (S4D with a pointwise encoder)",
  "family": "ssm",
  "family_label": "State-space and recurrent models (S4D diagonal state space)",
  "released": "r2",
  "assumption": "Memory is linear and time-invariant: each of 48 channels passes through a fixed filter made of 16 decaying, oscillating exponentials (S4D), i.e. a bank of linear reservoirs with learned time constants from days to years, applied the same way whatever the weather. Nonlinearity happens only between the filters. That is the unit-hydrograph / linear-reservoir view of a catchment, stacked three deep. The released rung adds a pointwise nonlinear encoder before the first filter, so the day's inputs can be partitioned (rain vs snow, thresholds) before they enter the linear memory.",
  "core": "Linear 29 -> 48 channels, a residual pointwise MLP (48 -> 48 -> 48, GELU; added in r2), then 3 S4D layers (pre-LayerNorm; per channel 16 complex diagonal modes, S4D-Lin initialisation, zero-order hold with a learned step in 1e-3..1e-1, the 730-day kernel applied by FFT convolution, skip term D; then GELU and a GLU channel mix, residual), final LayerNorm, dropout 0.1 before the head. numpy builds the same kernel and convolves by FFT (length 2048, exact linear convolution).",
  "rungs": [
   {
    "id": "r0",
    "change": "base: S4D (3 layers, 48 channels, 16 complex modes each), FlowCast's training recipe (3000 steps)",
    "decision": "base",
    "why": "first rung."
   },
   {
    "id": "r1",
    "change": "2x training: 6000 steps",
    "decision": "rejected",
    "why": "sim +0.001 (0.399 vs 0.398): unlike the LSTM, TCN and transformer, the linear time-invariant SSM gains nothing from more training (dev 0.428)."
   },
   {
    "id": "r2",
    "change": "pointwise nonlinear encoder before the SSM layers (residual MLP 48 -> 48 -> 48, GELU); 30.4k parameters; 6000 steps",
    "decision": "released",
    "why": "sim 0.422 vs incumbent r0 0.398 (+0.024; r1 shows the 6000 steps alone add nothing) and dev 0.435 >= 0.95 x 0.410: kept. Released (r3 did not beat it). A nonlinearity BEFORE the linear memory (e.g. rain vs snow, thresholds) is what the fixed filter lacked."
   },
   {
    "id": "r3",
    "change": "Mamba-lite: replace the S4D layers by selective state-space layers (input-dependent step dt and input/output maps B, C; scalar decay per head, 6 heads x 8, state 16; Mamba-2 style, gated output; no pre-MLP); 28.9k parameters; 6000 steps",
    "decision": "rejected",
    "why": "sim 0.415 vs incumbent r2 0.422 (-0.007; dev 0.429 vs 0.435). Selectivity beats the plain fixed filter (r1 0.399) but not the fixed filter with a nonlinear encoder in front. torch's chunked SSD equals numpy's day-by-day recurrence to 1.6e-15 (float64)."
   }
  ],
  "limits": [
   "Ungauged only: it never reads streamflow.",
   "Memory is limited to the 730-day window (each 365-day block gets its own 365-day warm-up).",
   "The S4D filter is the same for every day: how strongly a storm becomes flow cannot depend on how wet the basin already is except through the nonlinear layers between filters. The pointwise encoder (r2) fixes the input side only.",
   "Dev seed noise (measured on the LSTM control) is ~0.02 sd: dev differences under ~0.04 between arms are not meaningful.",
   "Bands are raw pinball quantiles (no dev widening)."
  ],
  "notes": [
   "Training cost per step: S4D about 0.5 s on one core (FFT convolutions), Mamba-lite 0.8 s; numpy inference ~0.017 s per 365-day block.",
   "numpy vs torch float32: ~3e-5 of the mean flow (torch's float32 complex exponentials over 730 lags); numpy equals torch float64 to 8e-15.",
   "The quick validation printed during training (first 200 val runs) is monitoring only; every decision uses harness evaluate.py on all 800 runs + the dev gate."
  ]
 }
